<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- THE PLUGIN SIDE of the JIT-plugin loader's pin: an ordinary JIT class library, exactly as a third party would
       write one. It declares its OWN copy of the table (that is what a C ABI means: the layout is the contract, not a
       shared C# type), it ships its own runtimeconfig.json, and it is loaded by an AOT host through nethost/hostfxr.
       `src/build/jitplugin-check.sh` builds it and stages it beside the host.

       THE RUNTIMECONFIG IS HAND-WRITTEN, because a class library build writes none and the loader starts the
       plugin's runtime from THAT file. It names `Microsoft.NETCore.App` 11.0.0 with `rollForward: latestMajor`, so
       the version in it is a FLOOR rather than a pin: a machine carrying a newer runtime runs this plugin without a
       rebuild, while a machine with only an older one is still refused by name instead of half-started. JSON takes
       no comments, which is why the reason lives here.

       WHY NOT `netstandard2.1` (asked for, and measured twice): that surface has no
       `UnmanagedCallersOnlyAttribute` (CS0246) and no unmanaged function pointers (CS8889, "the target runtime does
       not support default/extensible calling conventions"), which are the two things this plugin IS: the entry point
       `load_assembly_and_get_function_pointer` resolves and the table it fills. It also has no framework to start,
       so a plugin built that way would still need a config naming a concrete runtime. The framework version is the
       one precise number the contract cannot drop; its ROLL-FORWARD is what is up to the plugin, and it is wide. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AssemblyName>JitPluginFixture</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>JitPluginFixture</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
